const SITE_TEXT = {"rome": {"name": "Rome", "place": "Italy", "date": "Colosseum, 80 CE", "fact": "The Colosseum opened in 80 CE and could hold about 50,000 people watching games and shows. Roman engineers also built roads, bridges and aqueducts that carried fresh water into the city.", "more": "Rome grew from a small town into an empire all around the Mediterranean Sea. Romans built more than 80,000 km of paved roads, which is why people said “all roads lead to Rome.” The Romans' language, Latin, grew into Italian, Spanish, French, Portuguese and Romanian.", "hello": "Salve! That means hello in Latin.", "guide": "Marcus", "title": "Legionary and Road Builder"}, "athens": {"name": "Athens", "place": "Greece", "date": "Parthenon, 447–432 BCE", "fact": "Athens is where democracy began, around 508 BCE: citizens voted on the laws themselves. The Parthenon, a marble temple to the goddess Athena, stands on a rocky hill called the Acropolis.", "more": "Athenians met on a hill called the Pnyx to debate and vote. But only free adult men who were citizens could vote. Women, enslaved people and foreigners could not. Athens also gave the world famous plays and thinkers like Socrates. The olive tree on the Acropolis was sacred to Athena.", "hello": "Chaire! Welcome to Athens.", "guide": "Thalia", "title": "Teacher of Athens"}, "giza": {"name": "Pyramids of Giza", "place": "Egypt", "date": "c. 2560 BCE", "fact": "The Great Pyramid was built about 4,500 years ago as a tomb for Pharaoh Khufu. It is the only one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World still standing. The Great Sphinx guards the pyramids.", "more": "Tens of thousands of workers built the pyramids. Archaeologists found the workers' town nearby, with bakeries and breweries — workers were paid partly in bread and beer! The pyramids were once covered in smooth white limestone that shone in the sun.", "hello": "Welcome, traveler, to the land of the Nile!", "guide": "Merit", "title": "Scribe of Giza"}, "alexandria": {"name": "Alexandria", "place": "Egypt", "date": "Founded 331 BCE", "fact": "Alexander the Great founded Alexandria in 331 BCE. Its lighthouse, the Pharos, was one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World, and its Great Library collected books from all over the known world.", "more": "The Great Library may have held hundreds of thousands of scrolls. A librarian named Eratosthenes even measured the size of the Earth, using shadows and math! The Pharos lighthouse was about 100 meters tall, with a fire at the top to guide ships.", "hello": "Welcome to Alexandria, city of books!", "guide": "Demetra", "title": "Librarian of Alexandria"}, "babylon": {"name": "Babylon", "place": "Iraq", "date": "Ishtar Gate, c. 575 BCE", "fact": "Babylon stood on the Euphrates River in Mesopotamia, today's Iraq. King Nebuchadnezzar II built the Ishtar Gate around 575 BCE, covered in shiny blue bricks with golden bulls and dragons. The tall stepped tower is a ziggurat, a temple.", "more": "King Hammurabi of Babylon wrote one of the oldest law codes, around 1754 BCE: 282 laws carved on a stone pillar. People in Mesopotamia also invented writing. They pressed wedge-shaped marks, called cuneiform, into wet clay tablets.", "hello": "Peace be with you! Welcome to Babylon.", "guide": "Iltani", "title": "Babylonian Scribe"}, "persepolis": {"name": "Persepolis", "place": "Iran", "date": "Begun c. 518 BCE", "fact": "Persepolis was a capital of the Persian Empire, begun by King Darius I around 518 BCE. People from all over the empire climbed its grand stairs to bring gifts to the king in a great hall of columns called the Apadana.", "more": "The Persian Royal Road ran about 2,700 km. Royal messengers changed to fresh horses at stations along the way and could carry a message the whole distance in about a week. Walking it took about three months!", "hello": "Greetings! You've come a long way.", "guide": "Arta", "title": "Royal Road Messenger"}, "mohenjoDaro": {"name": "Mohenjo-daro", "place": "Pakistan", "date": "c. 2500 BCE", "fact": "Mohenjo-daro was one of the world's first big planned cities, built around 2500 BCE in the Indus Valley. It had straight streets, brick houses, covered drains, and a big public pool called the Great Bath.", "more": "Indus cities used bricks of the same shape and weights of the same size, so people over a huge area agreed on measurements. Their seals show animals like a one-horned 'unicorn'. Their writing still hasn't been decoded — so we don't even know what they called themselves!", "hello": "Welcome! Our writing hasn't been decoded yet, so nobody today knows the names we used. Just call me Potter.", "guide": "Potter", "title": "Indus Craftsperson"}, "teotihuacan": {"name": "Teotihuacan", "place": "Mexico", "date": "c. 100–550 CE", "fact": "Teotihuacan, in today's Mexico, was one of the biggest cities in the world around 500 CE. The Pyramid of the Sun faces the Avenue of the Dead. The Aztecs found the city in ruins centuries later and gave it its name.", "more": "Teotihuacan was a busy trading city. Its workers made sharp tools and blades from obsidian, a black volcanic glass, and traded them far away. Families lived together in big stone apartment compounds.", "hello": "Welcome to the City of the Gods!", "guide": "Citlali", "title": "Obsidian Trader"}, "greatWall": {"name": "Great Wall of China", "place": "China", "date": "221 BCE – 1644 CE", "fact": "The Great Wall is really many walls, built over about 2,000 years to protect China's northern borders. The first emperor, Qin Shi Huang, joined older walls around 221 BCE. Most of the stone wall you see today was built by the Ming dynasty.", "more": "Soldiers lived in watchtowers along the wall. When they spotted raiders, they lit fires at night or sent up smoke by day, passing the warning from tower to tower. The Silk Road traders passed its western end.", "hello": "Halt! Oh — a friend. Welcome to the wall.", "guide": "Wei", "title": "Wall Guard"}, "angkor": {"name": "Angkor Wat", "place": "Cambodia", "date": "c. 1113–1150 CE", "fact": "Angkor Wat in Cambodia is the largest religious monument in the world. The Khmer king Suryavarman II built it in the 1100s CE. Its five towers are shaped like lotus buds, and a wide moat surrounds it.", "more": "Angkor was a huge city with reservoirs and canals that stored water for rice fields. Angkor Wat was built as a Hindu temple and later became Buddhist. Its walls are carved with over 1,700 dancing figures called apsaras — and it's on Cambodia's flag!", "hello": "Sues-dei! Welcome to Angkor.", "guide": "Sophea", "title": "Temple Builder"}, "atlantis": {"name": "Atlantis (Legend)", "place": "A story, not a place", "date": "Told c. 360 BCE", "fact": "LEGEND: Atlantis comes from a story by the Greek philosopher Plato, written around 360 BCE. He described a mighty island that sank into the sea. Historians and archaeologists have found no evidence that it was real.", "more": "I wrote about Atlantis in my books Timaeus and Critias, to show how pride can bring down even a powerful nation. Some people later believed it was real. But it was a story to teach a lesson, not a history book. These ruins are only make-believe, for the game!", "hello": "Ah, a visitor! Sit, and let us think together.", "guide": "Plato", "title": "Greek Philosopher"}};
const CITY_INFO = {"rome": {"population": 1000000, "when": "around 100 CE", "style": "roman", "life": "Most of us live in apartment blocks called insulae, some six or seven floors high! Few flats have kitchens, so we buy hot food at street counters, and everyone goes to the public baths.", "names": ["Lucius", "Julia", "Gaius", "Cornelia", "Titus", "Livia", "Quintus", "Claudia", "Sextus", "Aurelia", "Decimus", "Flavia"], "jobs": [["baker", "I bake bread in a big stone oven. Rome gives free grain to its poorer citizens, so bakers are always busy."], ["potter", "I make amphorae, tall jars for wine, olive oil and fish sauce. Ships carry them all over the empire."], ["stonemason", "I cut stone for roads and aqueducts. Romans invented a concrete so strong that some of it still stands today."], ["shopkeeper", "My shop opens onto the street under the apartments. Nearly every ground floor in Rome is a shop!"], ["water carrier", "Eleven aqueducts bring fresh water into Rome from the hills. I carry it from the public fountain up to the top floors."]]}, "athens": {"population": 150000, "when": "around 430 BCE", "style": "greek", "life": "Our houses are built around a small open courtyard. Boys go to school to learn reading, music and sport. Everybody meets in the agora, the marketplace, to shop and argue about politics!", "names": ["Nikias", "Phoebe", "Theron", "Ariadne", "Kleon", "Daphne", "Lysander", "Iris", "Myron", "Chloe"], "jobs": [["olive farmer", "Olive oil is for cooking, for lamps and even for washing. The olive tree was Athena's gift to our city."], ["vase painter", "I paint black and red pictures of heroes and athletes on pots. People will dig them up thousands of years from now!"], ["sculptor", "I carve marble statues. We painted them in bright colours, even though the paint has worn off the ones you see in museums."], ["rower", "I row in a trireme, a warship with 170 rowers. Many rowers were poor citizens, and they could vote in the Assembly too."]]}, "giza": {"population": 20000, "when": "around 2500 BCE", "style": "egyptian", "life": "We pyramid builders live in a town with bakeries, breweries and even doctors. We eat bread, onions, fish and beef, and we're paid partly in bread and beer.", "names": ["Ankhu", "Nefret", "Senbi", "Meret", "Kha", "Tiye", "Ahmose", "Henutsen"], "jobs": [["stone hauler", "Teams of us drag huge blocks on wooden sledges. Someone pours water on the sand in front so the sledge slides more easily."], ["baker", "I bake bread in clay pots for thousands of workers. It's hot work!"], ["quarry worker", "We cut limestone blocks with copper chisels and wooden wedges."]]}, "alexandria": {"population": 500000, "when": "around 100 BCE", "style": "greek", "life": "Alexandria is full of people from everywhere: Greeks, Egyptians, Jews, Romans and more. Ships from all over the sea unload grain, papyrus and glass in our two harbors.", "names": ["Ptolemais", "Theon", "Berenike", "Sarapion", "Isidora", "Apollonios", "Hypatia", "Dion"], "jobs": [["papyrus maker", "I make paper from papyrus reeds that grow along the Nile. The Great Library needs a lot of it!"], ["sailor", "The Pharos lighthouse guides our ships home at night. You can see its fire from far out at sea."], ["glassblower", "Alexandria is famous for glass. I blow bottles and beads to sell all over the Mediterranean."]]}, "babylon": {"population": 200000, "when": "around 570 BCE", "style": "mesopotamian", "life": "Our houses are made of mud brick with flat roofs. On hot nights we sleep up on the roof where it's cooler. Scribes keep all the records on clay tablets.", "names": ["Ninsun", "Ahu", "Belessunu", "Tabni", "Ishme", "Amat", "Nabu-zer", "Ilta"], "jobs": [["brick maker", "We shape mud bricks in wooden molds and dry them in the sun. The best ones are baked in a kiln and glazed blue."], ["canal digger", "Canals carry water from the Euphrates to the fields. Without them, nothing would grow here."], ["astronomer", "We watch the stars and planets every night and write down what we see. We split the hour into 60 minutes!"]]}, "persepolis": {"population": 30000, "when": "around 500 BCE", "style": "persian", "life": "Persepolis is a royal city for ceremonies. Every spring, people from all over the empire come to bring gifts to the king for the New Year festival, Nowruz, which people still celebrate today.", "names": ["Arash", "Roxana", "Bardiya", "Atossa", "Farnaz", "Kambiz", "Parisa", "Dariush"], "jobs": [["stone carver", "I carve the rows of gift-bearers on the stairways: people from 23 nations, each in their own clothes."], ["gardener", "Persian kings loved gardens with water channels and fruit trees. Our word for a walled garden gave English the word 'paradise'!"], ["messenger", "I ride the Royal Road. Fresh horses wait at every station, so the king's news travels fast."]]}, "mohenjoDaro": {"population": 40000, "when": "around 2500 BCE", "style": "indus", "life": "Almost every house here has its own bathroom, with a drain into covered drains under the street — something many cities didn't have even 4,000 years later!", "names": ["Bead Maker", "Weaver", "Farmer", "Trader", "Seal Carver", "Builder"], "jobs": [["bead maker", "I drill tiny holes through hard carnelian stones to make beads. Traders take them as far as Mesopotamia."], ["weaver", "We grow cotton and weave it into cloth. The Indus people were some of the first in the world to do that."], ["seal carver", "I carve animals and our writing onto little stone seals. Nobody today can read the writing — maybe you'll be the one to work it out!"]]}, "teotihuacan": {"population": 125000, "when": "around 500 CE", "style": "mesoamerican", "life": "Families here live together in big stone apartment compounds, with many rooms around open patios. The walls are covered in bright painted murals.", "names": ["Tochtli", "Xochitl", "Cuauhtli", "Ollin", "Nochtli", "Yaotl", "Mazatl", "Itzpapalotl"], "jobs": [["obsidian worker", "I chip obsidian, a black volcanic glass, into blades sharper than steel. We trade them far and wide."], ["farmer", "We grow maize, beans and squash together in the same field. People call them the Three Sisters."], ["mural painter", "I paint the walls of the compounds with jaguars, birds and gods, in red, green and blue."]]}, "angkor": {"population": 750000, "when": "around 1150 CE", "style": "khmer", "life": "Our houses stand on stilts to stay dry when the monsoon rains flood the land. Only temples are built of stone — even the king's palace is wood!", "names": ["Sokha", "Chanthy", "Vibol", "Bopha", "Dara", "Kanha", "Rithy", "Sreymom"], "jobs": [["rice farmer", "Huge reservoirs called barays store water for our rice fields, so we can grow rice even in the dry season."], ["temple carver", "I carve apsaras, heavenly dancers, into the sandstone walls of the temples."], ["fisher", "The great lake, Tonle Sap, is full of fish. We dry and salt them to eat all year."]]}};

